═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
PART 3: ANALYSIS
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

3.1 WHAT WORKED
──────────────────────────────────────
✓ Gap detection — correctly identified low accuracy
✓ Tree generation — created valid .vsm tree
✓ Tree registration — registered in system
✓ Validation — checked syntax before registration

3.2 LIMITATIONS
──────────────────────────────────────
✗ Single gap type — only detected threshold_low_accuracy
✗ No dynamic modification — generates new tree, not modify existing
✗ No performance tracking — doesn't track generated tree performance
✗ No rollback — can't revert to previous tree

3.3 LESSONS LEARNED
──────────────────────────────────────
1. Gap detection is critical — identifies where to improve
2. Tree generation is straightforward — template-based approach works
3. Registration prevents duplicates — important for system stability
4. Validation is essential — prevents broken trees from entering system

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
